  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The index relies on land surface temperature as the primary proxy for heat exposure, which may not fully capture the ambient air temperatures experienced by residents.",
      "what": "The study co-produced the first Heat Vulnerability Index (HVI) for Nottingham, UK, by combining land surface temperature data with socio-economic indicators like unemployment, disability, and age. Working alongside the Blue-Green Nottingham Group, the researchers mapped the spatial distribution of heat exposure, sensitivity, and adaptive capacity, and used Projection Augmented Relief Models to visualize the results for stakeholders.",
      "why": "It demonstrates how incorporating local stakeholder knowledge and physical 3D visualization tools into index creation produces policy-aligned climate risk maps, moving beyond traditional, purely top-down academic vulnerability assessments."
    }
